      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I guess this makes sense for games inside of VRChat, but I just avoid those worlds. I really do like being able to take a close look at how game levels are built. World built inside a sphere or cube? I want to see if they're using the default skybox or not.\n\nAlso worlds that load or unload parts are usually interesting when the walls are peeked through.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2023-12-13T23:38:26.651Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "Short avatars can fit under smaller spaces, and tall avatars will have to duck under ceilings to fit under them.\n\nWalls already have colliders. I want what we have in neosvr, where when you move your head to a wall, it pushes your whole avatar away.\n\nThis could work well in hide and seek worlds, and with avatar scaling, this could be a lot more immersive.\n\nI imagine small players running through mazes, into mouse holes tom and jerry style. And I don't want to be able to glitch through walls to see what's on the other side. It allows a lot of cheating in games, and also spoils things you were going to find out later in story or exploration worlds like the backrooms.\n\nIt's a bug in my opinion, and it needs to be removed or updated to a more modern/immersive feature. Glitching through the ground is also a nono. (old picture taken from someone figuring out you can glitch through walls by moving your head through them)",
